---
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import DocsFigure from '../../components/DocsFigure.astro';
import { href } from '../../lib/catalog';
import { SORTS } from '../../scripts/chart/tree';
---

<Docs
  title="Reading the chart"
  lede="The Chart page shows the whole catalog as one tree, from the companies that own watch brands down to single reference numbers. This guide covers what the boxes mean and how to change what they show."
>
  <h2 id="tree">One tree, five levels</h2>
  <p>
    Everything on the <a href={href('/chart/')}>Chart</a> page sits in one tree: an <strong>owner</strong> holds
    <strong>brands</strong>, a brand has <strong>lines</strong> (such as the Submariner), a line has <strong>models</strong> (the
    Submariner Date), and a model has <strong>references</strong>, the individual reference numbers (126610LN).
  </p>
  <p>
    The page opens on brands and their lines. Each level is a column, so a column only ever holds one kind of thing. Each box prints
    what it holds, such as "11 lines · 29 models · 169 refs". To see who owns each brand, start <strong>Levels shown</strong> at Owners.
  </p>
  <DocsFigure src="chart-overview.png" alt="The chart as it opens: a column of brands with their logos, each beside a column of its lines." wide>
    The chart as it opens: brands, and their lines.
  </DocsFigure>
  <DocsFigure src="chart-owners.png" alt="Owners and their brands: a column of owning groups, each box beside a column of the brands it owns." wide>
    Started at Owners: each group beside the brands it owns.
  </DocsFigure>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>A group owned by another group</strong>, such as Bucherer under Rolex SA, sits inside its parent's box, indented.
      So does a <strong>brand owned by another brand</strong>, such as Caravelle inside Bulova.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>An independent brand is its own owner.</strong> It's drawn as one box across the Owner and Brand columns, marked
      Independent. When more than 24 are in view, they fold behind one row you can open.
    </li>
    <li><strong>A brand with several part-owners</strong> appears under each of them, but is counted once.</li>
    <li>Long lists show their first boxes and a <strong>+ N more · show all</strong> row.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="controls">The controls</h2>
  <DocsFigure src="controls.png" alt="The chart's controls: search, Countries, More filters, Group by, Levels shown, Sort by and Independent brands." wide />
  <p>Each control answers a different question:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Countries</strong> and <strong>More filters</strong> choose which brands to show.</li>
    <li><strong>Group by</strong> adds one level above the owners.</li>
    <li><strong>Levels shown</strong> chooses how much of the tree to show.</li>
    <li><strong>Search</strong> finds any one thing, at any level.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id="filters">Choosing brands</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Countries</strong> first asks what "country" means: where the brand was founded (origin), its owner's home, its headquarters,
    or where its watches are made. Each choice says how many brands have it recorded. Presets pick a region, such as Europe or Asia, and
    <strong>Not recorded</strong> can be picked like a country, to find brands still missing one.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>More filters</strong> picks market segments and whether a brand is still trading. <strong>Independent brands</strong> can hide
    brands with no owning group.
  </p>
  <p>
    When filters leave out some of a group's brands, the group stays as context and says how many are shown, such as "4 of 11 brands".
    A brand drawn only because a brand it owns is in view has a dashed edge.
  </p>

  <h3 id="levels">Levels shown</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Levels shown</strong> takes two picks: where to start and how deep to go. Brands → Lines is the opening view. Owners → Brands
    shows who owns what. Lines → References lists every reference by line. Each level in the menus says how many there are under the
    current filters. A level with none can't be picked, and if one empties, the chart stops at the deepest level that still has
    something in it.
  </p>
  <DocsFigure src="chart-levels.png" alt="Japanese brands from owners down to lines: Seiko Group's brands, with Seiko's lines in the third column." wide>
    Japanese brands, from owners down to lines.
  </DocsFigure>
  <p>
    Shown down to references, each model lists its references inside its own box. Where the Brand column isn't shown, lines and models
    carry their brand's name, since names like "Diver" repeat across brands.
  </p>
  <DocsFigure src="chart-references.png" alt="Rolex zoomed in, from lines down to references: each model's box holds a grid of its reference numbers." wide>
    Rolex, from lines down to references.
  </DocsFigure>

  <h3 id="group-by">Group by</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Group by</strong> adds a level above the owners: <strong>Country, then owner</strong> (using the meaning picked under
    Countries), <strong>Segment, then owner</strong>, or <strong>Era founded, then owner</strong>. Ownership is always shown below it.
  </p>

  <h3 id="sort">Sort by</h3>
  <p>The boxes in each column are ordered by:</p>
  <ul>{SORTS.map((sort) => <li>{sort.label}</li>)}</ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Most references</strong> ranks by references, then models, lines and brands. It measures how much of a brand has been
    catalogued here, not how big the brand is. The other orders print their figure in each box. Boxes with no figure go last and say so.
  </p>

  <h2 id="zoom">Zooming in</h2>
  <p>
    Click a box to zoom into it, and its name runs down the bar at the left. Click that bar or a name in the trail above the chart to zoom
    back out, or press the browser's Back button. The strip above the chart shows the box you picked: its facts, where it sits, and a
    link to its page.
  </p>
  <DocsFigure src="zoom.mp4" alt="A recording: the pointer clicks Swatch Group, the chart zooms into its brands, then into Omega and its lines, then back out." wide>
    Zooming into Swatch Group, then Omega, then back out.
  </DocsFigure>
  <p>
    The chart stays zoomed into the same thing when you regroup it or change the levels. If a filter takes it out of view, the chart
    zooms out to the nearest box that's still shown and says so.
  </p>

  <h2 id="search">Search</h2>
  <p>
    Search looks through owners, brands, lines, models and references. Reference numbers match without their spaces, dots and dashes, so
    "126610 ln" finds 126610LN. Picking a result zooms to it. If filters hide it, they're cleared, and a note says so.
  </p>
  <DocsFigure src="search.mp4" alt="A recording: typing 126610 ln into search, picking the reference, and the chart zooming to the Submariner Date with that reference marked." wide>
    Finding a reference by its number.
  </DocsFigure>

  <h2 id="outline">Outline</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Outline</strong> shows the same tree as an expandable list, with the same counts. Phones open on the outline. It also suits
    screen readers.
  </p>
  <DocsFigure src="chart-outline.png" alt="The outline view: a list of owners and independent brands, each with its counts." wide />

  <h2 id="keyboard">Keyboard</h2>
  <p>
    In Columns, Tab moves to the chart. The arrow keys then move between boxes: up and down within a column, left to a box's parent and
    right to its first child. Enter zooms in and Escape zooms out. In Outline, the arrow keys move between rows and open or close them.
  </p>

  <h2 id="links">Links</h2>
  <p>
    Everything you set is kept in the page's address, so a link opens the chart exactly as you left it. Values the page doesn't recognize
    are left out of the view, and a note names them.
  </p>
</Docs>
